---
layout: base.njk
navKey: home
title: Take your coding agent setup anywhere you work
description: OCP keeps your agents, skills, plugins, and settings in one simple source and syncs them across every machine. Open source. OpenCode first, more harnesses coming.
permalink: /index.html
---
{% from 'example.njk' import example %}
{% from 'config-machine.njk' import machine %}
{% set installCommand = 'curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nandorocker/ocp/main/install.sh | sh' %}
<main id="main" class="home" tabindex="-1">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <h1>Take your coding agent setup anywhere you work.</h1>
      <p class="hero-description">Forget the trail of folders and dotfiles. Keep your agents, skills, plugins, and settings in one simple source, and OCP generates the config and syncs it to every machine. <span class="hero-support">Currently supports OpenCode. More harnesses <span class="hero-coming">coming soon</span>!</span></p>
      {{ example('Install OCP · macOS / Linux', installCommand) }}
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="#getting-started">Install <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}">Read the docs</a></div>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-visual">
      <img class="hero-illustration" src="{{ '/ocp-network.png' | url }}" width="1448" height="1086" alt="An OCP hub connecting shared skills, plugins, agents, and subagents to a laptop, desktop, and server.">
    </figure>
  </header>

  <section id="welcome" class="home-section" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
    <h2 id="problem-title">Your agent setup shouldn't live in a pile of folders.</h2>
    <ul class="pain-points">
      <li>You tweak an agent prompt on your desktop, and your laptop is still running the old one.</li>
      <li>A new machine means an afternoon of hunting for dotfiles and half-remembered configs.</li>
      <li>You want to try a different set of skills or plugins, but swapping them in and out by hand is a chore.</li>
      <li>Your review agents and your build agents share one tangled global config.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="comparison">
      <div class="comparison-panel before">
        <h3>Without OCP</h3>
        <div class="drift-machines">{{ machine('Desktop', 'drifted-desktop') }}{{ machine('Laptop', 'drifted') }}</div>
      </div>
      <div class="comparison-panel after">
        <h3>With OCP</h3>
        <div class="ocp-source"><strong>Your OCP source</strong><div class="source-components"><span>Agents</span><span>Skills</span><span>Plugins</span><span>Settings</span></div></div>
        <div class="generation-flow"><span>Sync the source. Generate on each machine.</span><div class="generation-branches" aria-hidden="true"><span>↓</span><span>↓</span></div></div>
        <div class="generated-machines">{{ machine('Desktop', 'generated') }}{{ machine('Laptop', 'generated') }}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="directives-title">
    <h2 id="directives-title">OCP enforces three directives.</h2>
    <div class="directives">
      <article><span class="directive-symbol" aria-hidden="true">↳</span><h3>One source.</h3><p>Your agents, skills, plugins, and settings live in one place you can read and edit. Run <code>ocp apply</code> and OCP generates the config your harness uses.</p></article>
      <article><span class="directive-symbol" aria-hidden="true">⇄</span><h3>Every machine.</h3><p>Put the source in Git and run <code>ocp sync</code>. Your laptop, desktop, and remote dev box stay on the same setup.</p></article>
      <article><span class="directive-symbol" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span><h3>Any profile.</h3><p>Keep a profile for implementation, another for review. Switch the whole setup with <code>ocp use &lt;profile&gt;</code>.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="profiles" class="home-section feature-split" aria-labelledby="profiles-title">
    <div><h2 id="profiles-title">One setup. Different jobs.</h2><p>Keep implementation and review in separate profiles. Share the settings they have in common, and choose the agents and skills each job needs.</p>{{ example('Switch profiles', 'ocp use review') }}<a class="text-link" href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#profiles">Working with profiles →</a></div>
    <table class="profile-comparison">
      <caption>For example, two profiles for different jobs:</caption>
      <thead><tr><td></td><th scope="col">Implementation</th><th scope="col">Review</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Focus</th><td>Write code</td><td>Check changes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Agent</th><td>Implementer</td><td>Reviewer</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Skills</th><td>Coding</td><td>Code review</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p id="machines" class="machine-note">Need different settings on one machine? Add a per-machine override. <a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#machines">Across machines →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="agent-highlight" aria-labelledby="agent-title">
    <h2 id="agent-title">Let your coding agent set it up for you.</h2>
    <p>Your source is just files. Point your coding agent at it and ask it to add a skill, restructure a profile, or write a machine override. OCP takes it from there.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#agents">Ask a coding agent to help →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="getting-started" class="home-section" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <h2 id="start-title">Up and running in three steps.</h2>
    <p>Start with a local source, and add Git sync when you're ready for a second machine.</p>
    <ol class="start-steps">
      <li><h3>Install OCP</h3>{{ example('Install OCP · macOS / Linux', installCommand) }}<p class="small">The installer checks the release checksum and puts OCP in <code>~/.local/bin</code>. <a href="{{ '/quick-start/' | url }}#install">Installation details and PATH help</a> · <a href="https://github.com/nandorocker/ocp/blob/main/install.sh">Read the installer first</a></p></li>
      <li><h3>Set up your source</h3>{{ example('Set up OCP', '~/.local/bin/ocp setup') }}<aside class="callout backup-warning"><strong>Back up first.</strong> Keep a copy of your existing OpenCode configuration before setup. OCP preserves it for <code>ocp reset</code>, then manages the global configuration path.</aside><p>Follow the prompts to start a new source or bring an existing setup. Your source is the directory of files you'll edit and keep.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Check the result and start a session</h3>{{ example('Start using OCP', '~/.local/bin/ocp status\nopencode') }}<p>Setup generates and activates a profile. <code>ocp status</code> shows which source and profile you're using, and <code>opencode</code> starts your session. Prefer to build your profiles by hand? Follow the <a href="{{ '/quick-start/' | url }}">quick start</a>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <h2 id="faq-title">A few things before you comply.</h2>
    <details><summary>Does it overwrite my existing config?</summary><p>OCP preserves your existing OpenCode configuration, then manages the global configuration path. <code>ocp reset</code> detaches OCP and restores that original when available, leaving your source untouched. Still, keep your own backup before running setup.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do I need Git?</summary><p>No. Start with a local source. Add Git sync when you want to use another machine.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which coding agents does it work with?</summary><p>OpenCode today. Other harnesses are coming.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I read the installer before running it?</summary><p>Yes. It's <a href="https://github.com/nandorocker/ocp/blob/main/install.sh">linked here</a>, and it verifies the release checksum when it runs.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does <code>ocp sync</code> do to my repository?</summary><p>It brings changes between machines and renders your profiles. It can also commit and push, so <a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#machines">read how sync works</a> before using it on a repository you share.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What operating systems are supported?</summary><p>macOS and Linux. Native Windows isn't supported yet. See the <a href="{{ '/quick-start/' | url }}#install">installation section</a> for details.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is it free?</summary><p>Yes. OCP is open source under AGPL-3.0.</p></details>
  </section>

  <div id="questions" class="author-card">
    <img class="author-avatar" src="{{ '/nando-avatar.png' | url }}" width="400" height="400" alt="" decoding="async">
    <p class="closing">I'm <a href="https://nan.do">Nando Rossi</a>, the developer behind OCP. If you get stuck or have an idea, <a href="https://github.com/nandorocker/ocp/issues">open an issue</a>. I'd like to hear how it fits your setup.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="legacy-links" aria-label="Topics from the previous site"><a id="commands" href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#commands">Command reference</a></div>
</main>
